<!-- _class: section -->

# The five business flows

What end users actually do, day to day.

---

# Flow 1 — Statement to Cash

The flagship workflow. Lead with this in every conversation.

A carrier (Aetna, Cigna, MetLife, whoever) sends the brokerage a statement listing the commissions they're paying. Today the brokerage opens that statement and re-keys the data. With Unite:

- **Step 1** — Statement file lands in Unite (drop it in, or bulk upload a folder)
- **Step 2** — Unite identifies the carrier and applies a *template* (a saved recipe for that carrier's format)
- **Step 3** — Each row gets matched to a customer and a product — automatically when possible
- **Step 4** — A user reviews the matches in the *Review & Approve* screen and clicks Approve
- **Step 5** — Approved lines flow to the GL via the Accounting Hub integration

*PM translation:* "It turns a stack of PDFs into posted journal entries with one person reviewing the work."

---

# Flow 2 — Producer Compensation

How the brokerage pays its salespeople.

Producers (the salespeople) earn commission based on the policies they sold. The rules are intricate — different rates by product type, by year-1 vs renewal, by split arrangements when multiple producers worked the same account.

- **Producer Comp Rules** — the rate table, one rule per producer × product × renewal year
- **Split Intake** — who-gets-what-percent when an account has multiple producers
- **Compensation Actuals** — the calculated payable, by producer, by period
- **Payable to GL** — approved actuals flow to the GL as journal entries

*PM translation:* "Producer pay used to live in a spreadsheet that one person owned. Now it's an auditable system with workflow approval and version history."

---

# Flow 3 — Month-end Close

The three workflows that converge at the end of every month.

The brokerage's controller has to close the books. Unite gives them three tools:

- **Accruals** — estimated commissions for the month, computed from product-level data. The controller reviews + adjusts + approves.
- **Journals** — uploaded journal files from various sources (carrier reports, manual entries, integration output). Approve and they post to the GL.
- **Collections** — accruals that didn't materialize as cash. The user opens a *Collections Case*, documents the investigation, decides whether to write off. Approved write-offs generate a journal automatically.

*PM translation:* "Month-end close goes from a fire drill to a workflow — every step has an owner, every decision leaves a trail."

---

# Flow 4 — The Producer Portal

The external surface. A separate URL for the brokerage's producers and brokers.

Producers don't log into the back-office hubs. They log into the Portal:

- **See their splits** — the producer self-services their own split intake submissions
- **See their statements** — the carrier statements that named them as the producer of record
- **See their reports** — embedded PowerBI dashboards with their performance numbers

The Portal uses a separate sign-in flow because external users aren't on the brokerage's internal Azure AD.

*PM translation:* "Producers used to email asking 'what was my commission this month?' Now they self-serve."

---

# Flow 5 — Carrier Onboarding via Mapping Studio

The AI-driven workflow that differentiates ERP Unite.

Every carrier sends statements in a different format. New carrier? You need a template. Historically that's two days of analyst work. With *Mapping Studio*:

- **Drop the file in** — a new carrier's statement, in whatever format they use
- **AI suggests mappings** — Google Gemini analyzes the file, proposes "this column is the customer name, this column is the gross commission"
- **Reviewer approves** — accept the AI's suggestions, override the ones that aren't right
- **Template saved** — next month's statement from the same carrier uses the template automatically

*PM translation:* "The AI piece. It's the demo moment. Show this and you'll get the meeting extended."

