# Where Unite plays in the customer ecosystem

What it touches. What it doesn't replace.

---

# What stays. What changes.

The most common customer question: "what do we have to rip out?"

<div class="cols two">
<div class="card">

### **Stays untouched**
The customer's **AMS** (agency management system). Their **GL** (D365, NetSuite, etc.). Their **payroll** system. Their **BI / reporting**. Their **customer-facing systems**.

</div>
<div class="card">

### **Unite adds**
The **finance layer between them** — the workflow surface where statements get processed, cash gets applied, comp gets calculated, and journals get posted.

</div>
</div>

*PM talking point:* "Unite is additive, not displacement. Your AMS still owns producers and policies. Your GL still owns the ledger. Unite is the workflow surface in between."

---

# The systems Unite talks to

What's on the integration list, in plain language.

- **Accounting Hub (or D365 Finance)** — receives posted journals, cash applications, accruals, comp actuals
- **FinOps / Dynamics 365** — exchanges vendor master, employee master, fiscal periods, financial dimensions
- **Workday (if present)** — payroll-related data, time entries, project staffing
- **Mulesoft (or equivalent)** — feeds in product and customer master data from the AMS
- **Microsoft Entra ID** — sign-on for both internal users and external producers (dual-tenant model)
- **Google Gemini** — the AI behind Mapping Studio (cloud inference, audit-traceable)
- **PowerBI** — embedded reports inside the Producer Portal

*PM action:* know which of these the customer actually has. Integration scope is the #1 driver of engagement size.

